# My Left Foot!

A book that no one else could have achieved, unlike anything read before, has recently been published. The publisher is the Warburg company.

This book is not a distinguished account of warriors on a battlefield. It was written by a man whose life story contains, as its chapters, episodes of suffering like that of someone trapped in a storm at sea with no possibility of rescue. Every page of the manuscript was written by the author holding a pen between the toes of his left foot.

Read the sorrowful history of this writer, renowned for suffering. In 1934, he was born to a bricklayer in Dublin. His father had twenty-two children, and Christy Brown, a birthplace of sorrow, was the ninth. He was born into the world without good fortune, carrying a burden of suffering from birth. A mother naturally wishes her son to advance in life like others. That is the way of the world. Christy's history continually deepened the pain in that mother's thoughts.

The days he passed were beyond count, but each consisted of particles of pain. Christy was not a son who could say that today had passed happily and look forward to tomorrow. He depended upon the help of others. Because of a disorder affecting his brain, he suffered from paralysis.

A mother longs to hear the prattle of the child she bore. She waits to hear what her son will say. Time destroyed these cherished hopes. He was unable to speak. Christy would try anyway, as if to say, "How can I remain silent? Let me attempt to speak." The attempt produced only a harsh, unintelligible sound from his mouth. Though he could not speak, he rose intending to walk. That young heart wished to walk like other people. When he tried, all he could do was fall. His legs had lost the strength to support his body.

Toys bring delight to children's hearts. He could not experience even that small happiness. Christy tried to play with toys as other children leapt about and played. His hands refused to touch them. In this effort too he met only failure. When he was taken to a doctor, the doctor declared that he had a disorder of the brain. Hearing this, Christy's mother was distraught by her son's condition.

When six-year-old Christy saw a piece of chalk before him, he picked it up between the toes of his left foot and wrote upon a slate. This new act brought joy to his mother. She therefore taught him how to form letters.

Having learned to write with his left foot, he also drew a picture with it and won a prize. At the age of twelve, through his own effort, he changed the doctor's opinion. He learned to write words correctly. He then gave form to his imagination in the book *My Left Foot*. He wrote the first half of the manuscript himself and dictated the latter part to his brother.

At the age of eighteen, he went to a place printed in OCR as Lourdes. With the help of Dr Robert Collis, he also learned to speak well. He became skilled at operating an electric typewriter with one hand. Christy is reported to have told him, "My left foot was given to me by my mother." The book he wrote brought joy to a heart crushed by the burden of sorrow. *My Left Foot* stands ready to give everlasting fame to Christy, who regarded his troubles as dust.

(*Dravida Nadu*, date printed in damaged OCR as 12-2-22)

---NOTES---
The article's biographical details are translated as printed and are not silently reconciled with later accounts of Christy Brown's life. The place name “லெளடர்ஸ்” and the publication year require image or source verification.
